    "updated_at": "2026-06-01T21:17:42Z",
    "body": "It's hard to tell. Here's what I was able to find:\r\n - There's no MDN documentation: https://github.com/mdn/mdn/issues/394\r\n - Only Chrome/Edge pass WPTs (except for a few incidental passes): https://wpt.fyi/results/document-policy\r\n - Mozilla is neutral: https://github.com/mozilla/standards-positions/issues/327\r\n - WebKit's position is unclear. When document policy was worked on, there was no WebKit standards-positions repository, and Chrome's request for a position on `webkit-dev` went unanswered in https://www.mail-archive.com/webkit-dev@lists.webkit.org/msg29677.html [^1], although @rniwa said it was being evaluated.\r\n    - The OP of this issue speculatively lists https://github.com/WICG/document-policy/issues/34 and https://github.com/WICG/js-self-profiling/issues/33 as \"support\", but that's pretty unofficial. @annevk, I asked you about this https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/12026#issuecomment-3782126056, but we still need to hear back.\r\n\r\nSo for the time being, it appears to be single-implementer.\r\n\r\n[^1]: The official `lists.webkit.org` archive link in https://webkit.org/asking-questions/ is dead, so I had to pull the archive from a different source. I'm assuming it's up to date and that I didn't miss anything (like an actual published position).",
